206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Microfinance, a Financial innovation that emerged in the late 20th century has played a transformative role in fostering rural economic growth. In the context of developing economies, particularly in rural areas where traditional banking services often remain inaccessible, microfinance has emerged as a potent tool for financial inclusion. )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/E (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (1. Informal Credit Systems \(Pre-20th Century\): Before the formalization of microfinance institutions, communities often relied on informal credit systems. In many societies, individuals pooled resources or formed lending circles to support each other during times of need. Rotating Savings and Credit Associations \(ROSCAs\), where members contribute money to a common fund regularly, and each member takes turns receiving the total sum, are examples of such informal arrangements. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2. Credit Unions \(19th Century\): The concept of credit unions, cooperative financial institutions owned and operated by their members, gained prominence in the 19th century. These institutions focused on providing financial services to individuals with limited access to traditional banking. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (3. Grameen Bank \(1970s\): The modern microfinance movement is often associated with the establishment of the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh. Professor Muhammad Yunus, an economist, founded the Grameen Bank in 1976 to address the financial needs of poor rural women. Grameen Bank introduced the concept of microcredit, offering small, collateral-free loans to empower women and stimulate entrepreneurship in impoverished communities.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/E (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (4. Spread of Microfinance Models \(1980s-1990s\): The success of the Grameen Bank model led to the replication of similar microfinance institutions \(MFIs\) worldwide. Many countries, particularly in Asia, Africa, and Latin America, adopted and adapted microfinance models to suit their local contexts. Non-governmental organizations \(NGOs\) and donor agencies played a crucial role in promoting and funding microfinance initiatives during this period. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (5. Evolution of Microfinance Institutions \(1990s-2000s\): Microfinance evolved beyond microcredit to include a range of financial services such as savings, insurance, and remittances. This broader approach aimed to address the diverse financial needs of the poor. The microfinance sector saw the emergence of regulated microfinance institutions, as some countries developed legal and regulatory frameworks to govern microfinance operations. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (6. Challenges and Criticisms \(2000s-2010s\): Despite its successes, microfinance faced challenges and criticisms. Concerns were raised about high interest rates, over-indebtedness, and the sustainability of some microfinance models. The sector underwent a period of introspection, leading to efforts to improve consumer protection, transparency, and responsible lending practices.)
/Pg 11 0 R
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/E (The historical roots of microfinance can be traced back to various traditional informal lending practices and community-based financial arrangements. However, the modern concept of microfinance emerged in the mid-20th century, gaining momentum as a powerful tool for poverty alleviation and economic development. Here is an overview of the historical background of microfinance:)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (7. Inclusion in Sustainable Development Goals \(2015 Onward\): Microfinance is recognized as a valuable tool for achieving various Sustainable Development Goals \(SDGs\). Financial inclusion, poverty alleviation, and gender equality are among the areas where microfinance can contribute significantly. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Today, microfinance continues to play a crucial role in promoting financial inclusion, empowering women, and fostering economic development in both urban and rural settings around the world. The sector has diversified, with a focus on achieving a balance between social impact and financial sustainability.)
/Pg 12 0 R
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (RURAL BANKING)
/E (RURAL BANKING)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Rural banking is designed to cater to the financial needs of rural communities, particularly farmers, fisherfolk, cooperatives, and small rural enterprises. Rural banks provide a broader range of services compared to microfinance institutions, including agricultural loans, micro and small business financing, deposit products, remittance facilities, and insurance. They play a critical role in supporting rural development by improving access to credit, mobilizing local savings, and stimulating economic activities that enhance agricultural productivity and rural livelihoods.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Together, microfinance and rural banking complement each other in addressing financial exclusion. Microfinance targets the poorest households and micro-entrepreneurs through small, flexible financial services, while rural banking serves the wider rural economy with more structured and diverse financial products. Both contribute not only to individual economic empowerment but also to broader community development and national goals of inclusive growth )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
