257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (These are specialized financial institutions that provide microfinance services. MFIs may offer a range of financial products, including microloans, savings accounts, and micro-insurance. They often operate as non-profit organizations or social enterprises.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Microfinance institutions \(MFIs\) aim to reduce poverty and promote financial inclusion by providing access to credit, savings, insurance, and financial services to underserved populations. They empower individuals, particularly women, to overcome social and financial barriers, start or expand small businesses, and improve their socio-economic status. MFIs also encourage savings mobilization, support access to education and healthcare, and provide financial literacy training to help clients build resilience and make informed decisions. In addition, they contribute to broader social and economic development by creating jobs, enhancing infrastructure, and strengthening communities, while also offering risk-mitigation tools such as insurance against illness or crop failure. Although guided by social missions, MFIs pursue financial sustainability to cover operational costs, expand outreach, and work with governments, NGOs, and donors to achieve long-term financial inclusion.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
